<h2>Two years at the Council, one hearing</h2>
<p>The supplier&rsquo;s reference reached the Delhi Facilitation Council in September 2023. A single hearing followed in December 2023. After that: neither the conciliation that Section 18(2) contemplates, nor the arbitration that Section 18(3) mandates if conciliation fails, nor even an appointment. For a micro enterprise carrying unpaid invoices, the statutory fast track had become a siding.</p>
<p>The supplier moved the High Court under Section 11(6) of the Arbitration and Conciliation Act, 1996. The buyer raised two objections — jurisdiction and prematurity — and both failed.</p>

<h2>Objection one: the Bangalore clauses</h2>
<p>The Principal Agreement fixed a Bangalore seat and an exclusive Bangalore forum. On ordinary arbitration principles those clauses would have sent the petition south. But the MSMED Act is not an ordinary overlay. Following <em>Gujarat State Civil Supplies Corporation v. Mahakali Foods</em> and <em>Harcharan Dass Gupta v. Union of India</em>, the Court held that Section 18 — fortified by Section 24&rsquo;s overriding effect — prevails over the contractual arbitration mechanism, and that Section 18(4) anchors jurisdiction at the supplier&rsquo;s location. The supplier being registered in Delhi, Delhi had jurisdiction. The statutory policy is deliberate: an MSME&rsquo;s remedy sits where the MSME is, not where the buyer&rsquo;s standard-form contract puts it.</p>

<h2>Objection two: &ldquo;conciliation first&rdquo;</h2>
<p>The buyer&rsquo;s second argument had surface appeal — Section 18&rsquo;s architecture does place conciliation before arbitration. The Court&rsquo;s answer, resting on <em>Vallabh Corporation v. SMS India</em> and the Bombay High Court&rsquo;s <em>Microvision Technologies</em>, was that the sequence binds the Council, not the supplier&rsquo;s access to justice. Where the Council never conducts conciliation, the supplier cannot be met with the plea that an unperformed stage bars the next one. Court-annexed mediation had, in any event, been attempted and failed with a &ldquo;Not-Settled&rdquo; report in February 2026.</p>

<h2>What this unlocks for MSME practice</h2>
<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>1. Diarise the Council&rsquo;s timeline.</strong> Section 18(5) contemplates disposal of references within ninety days. Once a reference visibly exceeds it with no movement, the record of that inertia becomes the foundation of relief.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>2. Write, then move.</strong> A letter to the Council seeking conciliation or appointment — unanswered — makes the inertia documentary. The Section 11 petition then presents a clean narrative.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>3. Plead the statutory override.</strong> Seat, venue and exclusive-forum clauses in the buyer&rsquo;s contract do not control; Sections 18(4) and 24 MSMED do. Plead supplier location and Udyam registration with precision.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>4. File the full contract.</strong> The Court&rsquo;s admonition on the unfiled Principal Agreement is a standing instruction: Section 11 petitions carry a duty of full disclosure, and incomplete records invite worse than deprecation.</div>
</div>
<div class="note">The reference went to the Delhi International Arbitration Centre, with all merits left open. Nothing in the order prejudges the invoices; it decides only that the dispute will finally be heard — which, for a micro enterprise financing its receivables, is most of the battle.</div>
